{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1474640-why-13-9-5","id":1474640,"title":"Why 13/9/5?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-02-14T12:54:29-08:00","id":1474640,"title":"Why 13/9/5?","content":"<br /> Is there a compelling reason? Abilities are capped at 3 before freebies, so what's wrong with &quot;26 points anywhere&quot;? <br /> <br /> (further: While 7/5/3 in attributes stops you from creating overpowered monsters, it/s really just the 7 that does that, and I suppose allowing yourself to put a 1 in something allows you to beef up the other two too much. What's wrong with 7/4/4 or 7/6/2 or 6/6/3 or even 5/5/5?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-14T14:08:21-08:00","id":1474645,"title":"","content":"It's hard to many any sort of objective case for the specific numbers chosen. AFAIK none of the original designers ever really explained why it started as 7/5/3 and 13/9/5 and the general answer of what it stayed that way for the WoD 20 boks is because if it wasn't broke don't fix it.<br /> <br /> The biased silos of points is easier to address. It does two things &quot;have a giant pile of points,&quot; doesn't do:<br /> <br /> 1) Reduces analysis paralysis. Its not actually about how powerful you characters can be (you can easily make a character with Attribute 5 + Ability 5 in whatever you want their best roll to be), it's about structuring character creation into smaller chunks so each is easier to digest. This is something that came out of late 80s game designs similar to the WoD where they did exactly what you're talking about. Especially in the era before everyone had computers in their pockets that could run char-gen programs to track all their dots, the &quot;one big pool of points&quot; approach was considered very cumbersome and a turn off to new/casual players. Why not 15 dots and 26 dots? Because it overwhelmed people new to the system, and getting the math write with pencil and paper was a pain. It's a lot more intuitive to subjectively rank three broad categories, and then get a small easier to track pile of dots for each one.<br /> <br /> Look at the successor systems: CofD does it the same way as the WoD, but the vast number of Merits is considered a weak point in char-gen because it lacks this structure (though worse because of an ever larger possible array of places to put dots). Exalted does silos for Attributes, but a big pile for Abilities; of course very few Exalted fans thing the char-gen system is good. Exalted Ability dot assignment is so constrained by Charm selection that basically by the time you take the dots you want to cover the Charms you want to start with, most of your dots are decided anyway. V5 and Exalted: Essence both use arrays as a different method of addressing the same problem. Storypath games (usually) use a more &quot;life-path&quot; style of silos where your starting Ability dots are largely determined by your Paths to make the same basic process feel more organic.<br /> <br /> Big old pile of dots really isn't where things are headed, even if the X/Y/Z model is increasingly a relic of older designs.<br /> <br /> 2) It purposefully creates lopsided but not min-maxed default characters. Or, in other words, the concept was to encourage specialization without compromising the freedom of being able to put dots where you want to put them, and without players skipping a few dots in stuff that they should probably take a 1 or 2 in because they want 3s in all their good stuff (see the Exalted point above and how that would apply to Disciplines/Gifts/etc. too). The system ensures some level of general competency, but still encourages characters that are pretty good at stuff do. With the giant pile approach, there's a lot of chances for characters to go too far in either direction.<br /> <br /> -----------------<br /> <br /> Something interesting, if anecdotal: A lot of people in the 00s (for both WoD and CofD) starting doing &quot;big pile of dots&quot; methods; esp. translating everything to XP to avoid the LCQA traps. Almost every group that tried it found that it largely resulted in very similar starting level characters to the RAW; even with new players that weren't biased by previous experiences with the RAW. CofD 1e Merits tended to be the big exception here.<br /> <br /> Basically, for the most part, it's faster, and easier, to just tell players to use the RAW char gen and then be open to some golden rule application for things like letting someone start with 6/5/4,or 11/10/6 for the very few cases where it would really sell the concept at start. Esp. when you consider having to filter out people that dump stat like crazy. when allowed.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}